# Normalise line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf is on for this working tree, so files land as CRLF on Windows. That is fine
# for source Go and JS, but NOT for anything a POSIX shell executes: GitHub Actions runs the
# `run:` blocks in these workflows through bash on Linux runners, where a trailing \r is a
# syntax error ("unexpected token `$'do\r'`"). Pinning them to LF makes the checkout correct
# regardless of who committed from where.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# JSON is compared BYTE FOR BYTE by four gates: the rule-format schema golden, the shared
# validation fixture, `rohyctl format --check` over the built-in rules, and the generated JSON
# Schema drift check. GitHub's Windows runners check out with core.autocrlf=true, so without
# this pin those files arrive as CRLF while the generators emit LF — and every one of those gates
# fails on Windows only. That is exactly how the v0.2.0 release build broke: macOS and Linux
# passed, Windows failed on TestSchemaGolden, and the release job was skipped.
*.json  text eol=lf
